## G. Shared module browser interaction contract

Every applicable module must use a consistent selection/detail layout:

1. Module selector with tooltip/accessible name.
2. Title and description.
3. Category/pack filters with active-state styling and item counts derived from actual data.
4. Search field with clear button, debounced filtering, keyboard support and useful no-results state.
5. Sort options where meaningful (name, recently added, favorites, newest).
6. Card grid/list with selected state; no click target without action.
7. Detail panel with title, tags, pack, capability/status, asset type, duration, resolution, frame rate, license/origin metadata when known, favorite action and preview.
8. Preview area with actual content, loading state, error fallback, play/pause when animation/audio supports it, and `Template` / `Your edits` comparison for templates that truly support configurable variants.
9. A primary action appropriate to the item (`Apply`, `Add to comp`, `Add glass panel`, `Open as new comp`, `Download`, or `Preview`), plus secondary actions such as `Reset values`, `Add to favorites` and `Remove from favorites` when applicable.
10. A setup/permission/connection notice if the action needs After Effects, a local binary, a missing asset, or a privileged role.
11. Confirm destructive or expensive operations. Preserve a clear Undo/reversal path for AE edits where technically supported.

Do not use one generic handler for all modules. Define typed action contracts per capability. The UI may reuse components, but each module must call the correct service and receive typed success/failure results.

