## J. Data model and Firestore design

Use stable document IDs, server timestamps, ownership fields, indexes for actual queries and collection-level documentation. Define explicit types in shared code. Use cursor pagination rather than loading unlimited collections. Avoid storing sensitive data in public-facing documents.

Suggested collections (adjust if the scaffold requires different naming, but retain these logical records):

1. **`users/{uid}`**
   - `uid`, `email`, `displayName`, `photoURL` (optional, provider-derived)
   - `role` (`user`, `admin`, `owner`)
   - `status` (`active`, `suspended`, `pending`)
   - `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `lastLoginAt` (only when recorded honestly)
   - `preferences` (theme, reducedMotion, previewSettings)
   - never accept role from public client writes.

2. **`modules/{moduleId}`**
   - stable id, label, description, icon key, route, display order
   - capability type, enabled state, required permission, desktop/dependency requirements
   - created/updated timestamps and schema version.

3. **`catalogItems/{itemId}`**
   - `moduleId`, `name`, `slug`, `summary`, `description`, `tags[]`, `categoryId`, `packId`
   - `assetKind`, `capabilityType`, `previewAssetId`, `sourceAssetId` (optional)
   - `metadata`: duration, width, height, fps, version, file size only where known
   - `supportedOperations[]`, `requiredHostCapabilities[]`, `requiredDependencies[]`
   - `licenseType`, `licenseNote`, `sourceType`, `sourceURL` (where legitimate and known)
   - `visibility` (`draft`, `published`, `archived`), `createdBy`, timestamps, revision/version.
   - Add `referenceLabels[]` and `referenceReportedCount` only as documentation metadata; never confuse these with functioning items.

4. **`categories/{categoryId}`**
   - module, label, slug, order, visibility, timestamps.

5. **`assets/{assetId}`**
   - owner/visibility, storage key, public/private state, MIME type, byte size, checksum, dimensions/duration if inspected, preview location, license, processing state, createdAt.
   - No secret bucket credentials or unbounded direct download URL.

6. **`favorites/{uid}/items/{itemId}`** or an equivalent owner-scoped representation
   - user id, item id, createdAt. Enforce uniqueness/idempotence by deterministic ID.

7. **`projects/{projectId}`**
   - owner uid, title, description, asset reference, project-file status (`metadata-only`, `uploaded`, `linked-local`, `packaged`), file size, checksum, AE version if known, composition summary if inspected, source path excluded unless the user explicitly stores it locally and does not upload it, tags, createdAt/updatedAt/lastOpenedAt, archive/delete state.
   - Check owner access for every read/write.

8. **`jobs/{jobId}`**
   - owner uid, type (`caption`, `download`, `ae-operation`, `asset-process`), status (`queued`, `claimed`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled`, `needs-setup`, `expired`)
   - validated input schema/version, safe output metadata, progress only when reported by worker, createdAt/startedAt/finishedAt/updatedAt, attempt count, idempotency key, device id, error code and redacted message.
   - Do not store arbitrary executable code in a job payload.

9. **`devices/{deviceId}`** (sensitive server-only or carefully rule-gated)
   - owner uid, display label, OS, connector version, host version, capabilities, lastSeenAt, status, revokedAt, pairing metadata. Credential hashes only; never store plaintext long-lived tokens in readable documents.

10. **`devicePairingTokens/{tokenId}`** (server-only)
    - hash of one-time token, owner uid, expiresAt, usedAt, intended purpose. Expire quickly and invalidate after use.

11. **`auditEvents/{eventId}`**
    - actor id/role, action, object type/id, timestamp, result, requestId, minimal redacted metadata. Append-only for clients; server-controlled creation.

12. **`analyticsEvents/{eventId}`** or a safer aggregate system
    - event type, timestamp, pseudonymous uid where justified, module/action category, app version, coarse outcome. Do not capture project filenames, transcript text, URLs with query secrets, file contents, or private prompt contents. Provide consent/opt-out and retention policy.

13. **`appConfig/public`** and **server-only configuration**
    - Public safe configuration such as maintenance message and visible feature flags. Server-only limits and secrets are not in client-readable documents.

Use Firestore composite indexes as needed. Document required indexes and setup. Create secure Firestore rules and test them: users can read/update only allowed profile fields on their own profile; they cannot self-promote roles; users can access only their favorites/projects/jobs/devices; catalog read access depends on visibility; only admins manage published catalog; audit logs are not client-writable; pairing token collections are server-only; and sensitive jobs/devices are accessed via authorized server routes where practical.

If the AI Studio agent cannot configure Firebase or the user has not approved setup, make setup state visible and provide precise steps. Do not silently fall back to `localStorage` as if it were a cloud database. Local storage may only hold harmless UI preferences/cache.

## K. Authentication, authorization and session security

- Use Firebase Authentication with Google Sign-In as the first flow, subject to explicit Firebase setup.
- Distinguish **authentication** (who the user is) from **authorization** (what the user can do).
- Use least-privilege roles: `user`, `admin`, `owner`. The first owner must be bootstrapped through a documented, server-controlled one-time process or secure environment configuration; never grant owner to the first arbitrary visitor.
- For sensitive writes, verify the Firebase ID token server-side using the appropriate Firebase Admin/server mechanism supported by the runtime. Do not trust a client-supplied `uid`, `role`, `email`, `isAdmin`, or `ownerId`.
- Authorization matrix: users manage their own favorites/projects/preferences/jobs/devices; catalog is read-only for standard users except favorites; admin manages catalog/assets/users at restricted granularity; owner manages high-risk roles and system settings.
- Do not create open public registration that grants elevated role. A suspended user cannot create jobs or access protected resources.
- Handle login cancellation, popup blockers, expired sessions, revoked sessions, sign-out, redirect state, and permission denial gracefully.
- Keep session persistence deliberate; never put access tokens in query parameters or logs.
- Add rate limits for sign-in-dependent sensitive operations, pairing, job creation, media URL processing, and admin mutations.

