## W. Phase order and stopping rule

Do not attempt an uncontrolled, single-pass rewrite of every layer. Use the following release phases, keeping the app runnable after each phase:

**Phase 1 — Scaffold + visual shell:** establish design tokens, responsive navigation, all route placeholders, public landing, authenticated app shell, shared catalog card/detail layout, module metadata and original preview primitives. Seed only records whose labels and capabilities are clearly defined. Keep controls honest.

**Phase 2 — Core catalog + user state:** working search/filter/sort, favorites, details, real item counts, project library metadata, preferences, loading/empty/error states and persistence.

**Phase 3 — Firebase backend + auth/RBAC:** provision Firebase after approval; add verified auth, Firestore model/rules/indexes, server API layer, server-side authorization, user-scoped records, seed setup and tests.

**Phase 4 — Admin CMS + technical status:** catalog/asset CRUD, draft/publish/archive, user/role controls, audit trail, real aggregate metrics and safe health checks.

**Phase 5 — Desktop companion + AE integration source:** generate typed shared contracts, pairing lifecycle, job queue, local bridge, AE handlers for supported operations, connector status, install/docs, security tests. Mark host integration unverified until it has been tested in real After Effects.

**Phase 6 — Local media utilities:** captioning, dependency manager, depth feature gates and policy-safe downloader. Use real desktop jobs, not fake browser-side status.

**Phase 7 — production hardening:** accessibility, security, performance, SEO, tests, error handling, versioning, release checklist and deployment verification.

For each phase, finish with: (a) summary of implemented work, (b) changed files, (c) checks actually executed and their actual results, (d) features still unverified, (e) blockers requiring user action, (f) next phase prompt. Do not claim a phase is complete if its acceptance checks fail.

